From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 6 Jul 2026 06:18:15 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 05868/11362] [outer-loop] fix llama-cpp-python CPU-only build:
 real GPU offload was never happening

Investigated why exp5284 (SOTA runtime offload receipt repair) blocked
two downstream .483 tasks 6 times across repeated retries. exp5284 was
a genuinely honest, well-designed precondition check, not a bug -- it
correctly detected that all three mandated SOTA GGUF models showed
blocked_no_gpu_offload_evidence.

Root cause, confirmed directly: the installed llama-cpp-python (0.3.29)
was a CPU-only build. llama_supports_gpu_offload() returned False, and
GPU memory stayed pinned at exactly 4 MiB through model load +
generation for all three models despite n_gpu_layers=-1. A plain pip
install llama-cpp-python pulls a prebuilt CPU-only wheel from PyPI; the
project never rebuilt it with CUDA flags.

This likely affected every "live SOTA GGUF inference" artifact in this
project's history, not just exp5284 -- CPU inference on 26-35B
quantized models is slow enough to still clear the 60s
live_llm_inference duration floor, so nothing ever tripped
DURATION_TOO_SHORT despite silently never touching either idle RTX
3090.

Confirmed the CUDA toolchain was available (nvcc 13.3, driver 610.43.02
matching both GPUs) before proceeding. Rebuilt from source with
CMAKE_ARGS="-DGGML_CUDA=on -DCMAKE_CUDA_ARCHITECTURES=86" (compute
capability 8.6 for RTX 3090). Verified end-to-end, not just the
capability flag: loading gemma-4-31B-it Q4_K_M with n_gpu_layers=-1 now
shows nvidia-smi memory jump from 4 MiB to ~9.4GB/10GB split across
both GPUs, and real generation succeeds ("OK. OK. OK" from a
deterministic prompt).

Checked for regressions from the side-effect numpy bump (2.4.4->2.5.1):
full adversarial_verify test suite (284 tests) green, core carnot module
imports (models, pipeline, agentic) clean.

Documented in CLAUDE.md's Build Environment section (the install
command + verification steps for future sessions) and
ops/known-issues.md. Flagged as not yet durable: llama-cpp-python isn't
pinned in pyproject.toml at all, so a future venv rebuild will silently
reinstall the CPU-only wheel unless the CUDA build flags are reapplied.

